# E11 Predator-Prey, GUI version: the stub of the form prints the turn its text box shows, then the populations it loads
# from world-state.txt at the end; 20 x 20 grid, 200 turns, sequential in a random order. The draws come from the seed
# of the oracle, so the C# fixes the populations until the first insects leave: from then on, the C# Dictionary reuses
# their places and changes the order of the following turns, which the C++ does not reproduce (a known divergence of
# the library). The first insects born (ants, turn 3) act from turn 4, so the first four turns are the same for any seed.
# Compared: the turns shown, the populations of the first four turns exactly, then their number, populations that fit on
# the grid, and the end of the simulation.
ignore ^$
cover all
fact turn ^Turn (\d+) / 200$
fact finished ^Simulation finished\.$
fact header ^Doodlebugs\tAnts$
fact population ^(\d+)\t(\d+)$
same turn seq
same finished count
same header count
same population seq first 4
check population count == 200
check population max(0) <= 400
check population max(1) <= 400
